-      <p class="why-it-matters">A probability model assigns probabilities to events, and an event is a set of outcomes. For a finite experiment, every set of outcomes can be an event. For an infinite experiment, the collection of events has to be specified with care, and §4 raises, without settling, whether the collection of all subsets is too large to use. This lesson defines that collection, the σ-algebra, and proves the properties that the rest of the unit uses: closure under complements, finite unions, and countable unions and intersections. It also shows that finite closure is not enough.</p>
+      <p class="why-it-matters">A probability model assigns probabilities to events, and an event is a set of outcomes. For a finite experiment, any collection of sets of outcomes can be taken as the events. For an infinite experiment, the collection of events has to be specified with care, and §4 raises, without settling, whether the collection of all subsets is too large to use. This lesson defines that collection, the σ-algebra, and proves the properties that the rest of the unit uses: closure under complements, finite unions, and countable unions and intersections. It also shows that finite closure is not enough.</p>
